              "text": "When making eye contact with someone who has a \"lazy eye\" (the medical term is **strabismus**, where the eyes do not align in the same direction), it can sometimes feel confusing because you aren't sure where to focus. \n\nBecause people with strabismus are often highly aware and can feel self-conscious about their condition, handling eye contact smoothly is a great way to put both of you at ease. The most effective, respectful strategies to use include:\n\n### 1. Look at the bridge of their nose\nThis is the easiest and most widely recommended trick for making eye contact in any situation, but it is especially helpful here. \n* **How to do it:** Focus your gaze on the bridge of their nose, right between their eyes. \n* **Why it works:** To the other person, it will look exactly like you are making direct, natural eye contact. For you, it removes the distraction of having to choose which eye to look at.\n\n### 2. Identify and look at their dominant eye\nIf you prefer to look at an actual eye, look for their \"dominant\" or straight eye.\n* **How to do it:** Pay attention to which eye is tracking you and pointing straight ahead\u2014this is the eye they are actively using to look at you. Focus your gaze on that eye. \n* **Why it works:** Because they are using their dominant eye to see you, looking directly into it creates a genuine connection. \n\n### 3. Avoid the \"Ping-Pong\" effect\nThe most common mistake people make is letting their gaze dart rapidly back and forth between the person's eyes trying to figure out where they are looking. \n* **Why to avoid it:** This rapid shifting (or \"ping-ponging\") is highly noticeable and can make the other person feel self-conscious or uncomfortable. Once you choose a spot\u2014either the dominant eye or the bridge of the nose\u2014**stick to it**.\n\n### 4. Keep your eye contact natural\nDon\u2019t overcompensate by staring intensely. In normal conversation, people don't lock eyes 100% of the time. It is perfectly natural to look away occasionally when you are thinking, gesturing, or laughing, and then return your gaze. Apply the same relaxed rules of conversation here.\n\n### 5. Remember: They are looking at you\nIt helps to understand that even if one of their eyes is wandering, **they are actively looking at and listening to you**. Their brain is simply prioritizing the visual input from their dominant eye. Just focus on what they are saying, treat them exactly as you would anyone else, and let the conversation flow naturally.",
